course: dictionary
lesson: 10886
title: Violence and its two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Violence is a noun, and it has two meanings. The first is behaviour that uses physical force to hurt, injure or kill someone, or to damage something. The second is great force or strength, as in a storm. Look at the words around it to find out which meaning the writer means.
explain: «His dad turned the box over and showed him the little pictures that say why a game got its number: a fist for violence, a speech bubble for bad language, a spider for scary content.» The picture of a fist is a clue, because a fist is used for hitting. The other pictures show bad language and scary content, so violence here means hurting people.
explain: «The violence of the wind» tells you the wind was very strong, not that anyone was hurt. In real life, violence to people is never the right way to solve a problem. Talk, walk away and get a trusted adult to help. If violence happens at home, tell a teacher or a helpline.
fact: Violence means using physical force to hurt, injure or kill someone, or to damage something.
fact: Violence can also mean great force or strength, as in «the violence of the wind».
fact: Storms, volcanoes and earthquakes can erupt with great violence, and this is the second meaning, not the first.
fact: Age ratings such as ESRB and PEGI warn about violence, scary content and online contact.
fact: Violence is never the right way to solve a problem: talk, walk away and get a trusted adult.
exercise: «A game rated 18 can contain realistic violence, crime, strong swearing and adult scenes.» What does violence mean in this sentence? | Being very quiet | Great strength, like a storm | *Physical force used to hurt people | A type of music
exercise: Which phrase uses violence to mean great force? | Hitting and kicking someone | A rating of 18 on a game | A speech bubble on the box | *The violence of the wind
exercise: On the game box, which picture shows violence? | a spider | a question mark | a speech bubble | *a fist
test: If violence is happening at home, what should you do? | Hit back to stop it | Wait and hope it stops by itself | Keep it secret and hide | *Tell a teacher or a helpline
test: What is the best way to solve a problem without violence? | Shout louder until they give in | *Talk, walk away and get a trusted adult | Break something to show you are angry | Hit or kick the other person
exam: Age ratings tell you what is IN a game, such as violence, blood, scary bits and bad language. What do age ratings tell you about? | How long the game lasts | *What is in a game | Who made the game | How hard the game is
